course: everyday
lesson: 214
title: Family changes and grief
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Families change: a new baby arrives, parents separate, step-families form, and sometimes someone we love dies.
explain: Babies cry because crying is the only way they can tell us they are hungry, tired or uncomfortable, so we are patient.
explain: When parents divorce, a child should not be put in the middle; you can say you love both and do not want to choose.
explain: When someone dies, their body has stopped working forever and they cannot come back; death is not like sleep, and sleeping does not make anyone die.
explain: A boy in Boston feels it was his fault that his grandpa died because he had been cross with him; his mum explains that thoughts and wishes can never make someone die, and they make a memory box together.
fact: Babies cry to tell us they are hungry, tired or uncomfortable, because crying is the only way a baby can talk.
fact: Grandparents' stories about the old days are family history that only they can tell, so you listen and ask questions.
fact: When grandparents have different rules, you follow the rules of the house you are in, kindly.
fact: If one parent says bad things about the other, you can say you love both and do not want to choose; children should not be put in the middle.
fact: Blended families take time to work out, so step-brothers and step-sisters find things they all enjoy and give each other space.
fact: If you are adopted or in foster care, you share only what you want; your story is yours to tell or keep.
fact: After a pet dies, you wait a while and talk with your family before getting a new one; you need time to grieve, and a new pet does not replace the old one.
fact: When someone you love dies, it helps to ask questions, cry, talk about them and keep a memory box.
fact: Grief is normal, and sharing it helps.
fact: Death means the body has stopped working forever, and the person cannot come back.
fact: Sleeping does not cause death.
fact: Thoughts and wishes cannot make someone die, so it is never a child's fault.
exercise: Your baby sister cries a lot. What is true? | Crying is the only way a baby can tell us what it needs | She cries because she does not like you and wants you to go | Babies cry for no reason at all | She is crying to annoy the family
exercise: Why does a baby cry? | It may be hungry, tired or uncomfortable | It wants to watch TV with the rest of the family | It is practising to sing | It is being naughty on purpose
exercise: Grandma starts telling a story about when she was a girl. What do you do? | Listen and ask questions | Look at your phone until she stops | Tell her you have heard enough old stories | Change the subject quickly
exercise: Why are grandparents' stories precious? | They are family history only they can tell | They are written down in every library book anyway | They are always made up | They are only for adults
exercise: At Grandad's house you must take off your shoes, but at home you do not. What do you do? | Follow the rules of the house you are in, kindly | Keep your shoes on, because your home rules count everywhere | Argue that your parents allow it | Refuse to visit
exercise: Your dad says unkind things about your mum after the divorce. What can you say? | That you love them both and do not want to choose | That you agree mum is bad and you're on his side now | Nothing, and pick a side in secret | That you will only live with one of them
exercise: Why should children not be put in the middle when parents separate? | It is not their job to choose between parents they love | Children always choose wrongly | Children must decide where everyone in the family lives from now on | Parents need their children to take sides
exercise: You have new step-brothers who are very different from you. What helps? | Find things you all like and give each other space | Ignore them all until they move out or go away again | Compete with them all the time | Tell them the house is yours
exercise: You are adopted and a classmate asks lots of questions about it. What can you do? | Share only what you want to share | You must answer every question they ask you | Make up a false story to stop them | Get angry and push them away from you
exercise: Your dog died last week and you want a puppy at once. What is wise? | Wait a while and talk it through with your family | Get a puppy today so you can quickly forget the old dog | Buy one that looks just like your dog | Promise never to love a pet again
exercise: Your grandma has died. What can help you? | Ask questions, cry, talk about her and keep a memory box | Never mention her again, so that it does not hurt as much | Pretend nothing happened | Keep your sadness secret from everyone
exercise: Your little cousin asks if Grandpa, who died, is just asleep. What is true? | Death means the body stopped working forever and he cannot come back | He is asleep and will wake up later | He is on a long holiday | He can come back if we wish hard
exercise: A child is scared to go to sleep after hearing someone "went to sleep" and died. What is true? | Sleeping does not cause death | Sleeping is dangerous for children | Only long sleeps are dangerous | Children should not sleep at night
exercise: You were angry at your uncle and wished he would go away. Then he died. What is true? | Thoughts and wishes cannot make someone die | Your wish caused it, because you wished it so strongly | Angry thoughts can make people ill | You must keep this secret forever
test: A girl in Calgary gets cross when her baby brother cries at dinner. What would help her be patient? | Knowing that crying is the baby's only way to tell what he needs | Knowing that babies cry to annoy people | Shouting at the baby to stop | Leaving the house every evening
test: A boy's grandparents let him stay up late, but his parents do not. At his grandparents' house, what is right? | Follow the grandparents' house rules kindly | Ring his parents to complain about the new bedtime | Stay up even later | Refuse to go to bed at all
test: A girl's mum asks her which parent she likes more since the divorce. What is a good answer? | "I love you both and I don't want to choose." | "I like you best, because Dad is the bad one." | "I like Dad best." | Run away and not answer
test: Two step-sisters keep arguing about the TV. What is most likely to help? | Finding things they both enjoy and giving each other space | Making one of them always win | Never speaking to each other | Asking the parents to split up
test: A boy in foster care is asked by a new friend why he does not live with his mum. What is true? | He can choose how much of his story to share | He must explain everything, right from the very start | He must lie | He should never make friends
test: Your hamster dies, and the next day your parents offer a new one. You still feel sad. What is a good choice? | Say you want to wait a while and talk about it | Accept, since the new one replaces the old one | Pretend you are not sad | Refuse ever to talk about the hamster
test: A friend's dad has died. What can friends do that helps? | Let her talk and cry, and listen | Tell her not to be sad any more, it helps | Avoid her until she is cheerful again | Change the subject whenever he is mentioned
test: Why do adults use clear words like "died" with young children instead of "went to sleep"? | Clear words help children understand, and sleep words can make them afraid of sleeping | "Died" sounds kinder than "asleep" | Children cannot understand clear words | It does not matter which words are used
test: A girl believes her grandma died because she forgot to visit her. What should an adult tell her? | It was not her fault; nothing she thought or did made her grandma die | It was partly her fault | She should visit others to make up for it | Not to talk about it ever
test: Grandpa tells you about the farm he grew up on. Why ask him questions? | His stories are family history that only he can tell | So he gets to the end and stops talking a bit sooner | Because it is homework | Old stories are always made up
test: dad says bad stuff about mum, i dunno what to say | Say you love both and don't want to choose | Agree with whoever is talking at the time, it's easier | Tell mum everything he says | Stop speaking to both
test: my sister says nan died cos she went to sleep, true? | No, sleeping does not cause death | Yes, sleep can make old people just stop | Yes, so try hard to stay awake at night | Only a very long, deep sleep does that
test: i wished my grandad would go away and now he's died, my fault? | No, wishes can't make someone die; it's never your fault | Yes, wishes can come true | Yes, partly your fault | Only if you said it out loud
test: baby wont stop crying, why is he doing that | He's telling us he's hungry, tired or uncomfortable | He's being naughty on purpose to wind everyone up | He doesn't like you | Babies cry for no reason at all
explain: After his grandad died, a boy in Wales kept all his sadness inside and began getting stomach aches every day; when he finally talked with his mum and a school counsellor (a person at school whose job is to listen and help with feelings), the aches eased.
explain: When her parents split up, a girl in California believed it was her fault and stopped seeing her friends; it was not her fault at all, and talking to both parents helped her understand.
fact: Children who keep grief hidden often feel it in their bodies as headaches, stomach aches or trouble sleeping.
fact: When parents separate, it is never the child's fault, and children feel better when they can ask questions and talk.
fact: Grown-ups have gentle phrases for these things: «going our separate ways» means a couple is splitting up, and «the love of my life» means the person someone loved most.
test: grandad died, i don't wanna talk about it ever | It's okay to wait, but talking with someone you trust helps | Never talk, it makes it worse | Pretend it didn't happen | Only talk to your pet
test: Mia thinks her parents split up because she was naughty. Which is true? | It is not her fault; it is a grown-up decision | It is partly her fault for being naughty, but only a little bit | She can fix it by being extra good | Children usually cause their parents to split
test: mum says we're moving after the divorce, i'm so scared | Tell her how you feel and ask your questions | Run away so you don't have to move house at all | Keep quiet and hope it stops | Blame your sister
test: my cousin said a kid in her school wished his grandad would go away and then the grandad died that week, so it was his fault. true? | No, thoughts and wishes can't make anyone die | Yes, strong wishes can come true if you mean them hard | Yes, if he said it out loud | Partly, if he was very angry
test: apparently that boy's parents are splitting up cos he's so naughty, everyone's saying it. what's just a rumour? | That it's his fault; separation is never the child's fault | That his parents may be separating | That he might feel upset | That children feel better talking
test: did u hear the new girl's adopted, her real mum was in prison or something. should u spread it? | No, her story is hers to tell or keep | Yes, the whole class has a right to know | Yes, if you ask her first in front of others | Yes, adoption is nothing to hide anyway
test: overheard dad on the phone: "we're going our separate ways, it's for the best". what does it mean and who can you talk to? | Mum and dad may be separating; you can ask them or another trusted adult about it | Dad is going on a business trip | Mum and dad are walking home on two roads | It's your fault, so keep it secret forever
test: kid at school says "ur mum's got a boyfriend already, ur dad's only been gone a year, that's weird" — what's fair to think? | It's your mum's private life; the kid is being unkind, and you can tell a trusted adult | The kid is right, so be angry at mum | Hit the kid so he stops | Ask the whole class to vote if it's weird
test: grandad says "your nan was the love of my life, sixty years and never a cross word". what does "love of my life" mean? | She was the person he loved most | She was his favourite cook in the whole town | She was his very first teacher at school | She was his big sister growing up
exam: Identify the most accurate statement concerning the cause of the separation of parents. | It is never the fault of the child | It is generally the fault of the eldest child | It is caused by children's quarrels | It is caused by the child's poor school marks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises death? | The body ceases to function permanently and the person cannot return | It is a form of very deep sleep | The person returns after some time | It may be caused by a child's wishes
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of an adopted child's personal history. | The child shares only that which he wishes to share | The child is obliged to tell the whole class when asked to do so | Teachers must announce it to all pupils | It must be kept secret from the child
exam: Ascertain the bodily signs that may arise in children who conceal their grief. | Headaches, stomach aches or difficulty in sleeping | Improved appetite, longer sleep and a cheerful manner | Faster growth and stronger muscles | No signs whatsoever in any child
